(version 1)

# Eve's netclass rules.
#
# These rules are the PCB half of the netclass policy. They are useless unless the
# matching netclasses exist in the project and nets are assigned to them. Create the
# classes in Project > Project Settings > Net Classes first:
#
#   POWER      supply nets up to 2 A
#   HIGHPOWER  supply nets above 2 A (amplifiers, motors, lighting)
#   MIDFREQ    > 1 MHz square, > 2 MHz sine, or rise time < 400 ns
#   HIGHFREQ   rise time < 10 ns, > 50 MHz square, or > 100 MHz sine
#   SENSITIVE  enables, resets, chip selects, interrupt and fault lines
#
# Paste into <project>.kicad_dru, or keep this file and include its rules there.
# KiCAD has no include directive, so the rules must be copied in.
#
# WARNING: kicad-cli accepts a broken .kicad_dru in silence. A file with an
# unbalanced paren or a misspelled function reports the same violation count as
# a correct one. Never conclude a rule works because DRC ran. Validate by
# temporarily setting the constraint absurdly tight (clearance min 8mm) and
# confirming the violation count rises and names the nets you expect.

# --- Power: minimum copper width -------------------------------------------------

(rule "POWER minimum track width"
  (severity error)
  (condition "A.NetClass == 'POWER'")
  (constraint track_width (min 0.25mm))
)

(rule "HIGHPOWER minimum track width"
  (severity error)
  (condition "A.NetClass == 'HIGHPOWER'")
  (constraint track_width (min 0.4mm))
)

# --- Fast signals: clearance -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Eve's rule is "clearance greater than two or three times the track width". A DRC
# rule cannot read the local track width, so these encode a multiple of the assumed
# 0.15 mm netclass track width: 2x for MIDFREQ, 3x for HIGHFREQ. Adjust both numbers
# if the netclass track width is not 0.15 mm.
#
# These fire between a fast net and ANY other net, including another net of the same
# bus. If the design has real buses, replace the HIGHFREQ rule with the bus-aware
# version at the end of this file. Do not keep both: a flat HIGHFREQ clearance rule
# reports every intra-bus pair, and no amount of rule ordering will suppress it.

(rule "MIDFREQ clearance"
  (severity warning)
  (condition "A.NetClass == 'MIDFREQ'")
  (constraint clearance (min 0.3mm))
)

(rule "HIGHFREQ clearance"
  (severity error)
  (condition "A.NetClass == 'HIGHFREQ'")
  (constraint clearance (min 0.45mm))
)

# --- Fast signals: keep them direct ----------------------------------------------
#
# High-speed nets should not change layer. via_count counts vias on the whole net,
# so the limit is a budget, not a ban: raise it if the net legitimately spans
# connectors on two sides of the board.

(rule "HIGHFREQ via budget"
  (severity warning)
  (condition "A.NetClass == 'HIGHFREQ'")
  (constraint via_count (max 2))
)

# --- Fast signals: do not change width mid-course ---------------------------------
#
# Expressed as an exact width rather than a minimum, so a widened or necked section
# is reported. Set these to the real netclass widths of the design.

(rule "MIDFREQ constant track width"
  (severity warning)
  (condition "A.NetClass == 'MIDFREQ'")
  (constraint track_width (min 0.15mm) (max 0.25mm))
)

(rule "HIGHFREQ constant track width"
  (severity error)
  (condition "A.NetClass == 'HIGHFREQ'")
  (constraint track_width (min 0.15mm) (max 0.2mm))
)

# --- Sensitive lines: keep them away from fast nets -------------------------------
#
# A coupled spike on an enable, a reset or an interrupt is dire: the part shuts
# down, resets, or takes a phantom interrupt, and the fault is intermittent and
# very hard to find. These lines may take indirect paths, but not paths that run
# alongside something fast.
#
# Note the OR on the B side: B.hasNetclass is needed here because the test is
# "is the other item fast", which A.NetClass cannot express.

(rule "SENSITIVE clearance to fast nets"
  (severity warning)
  (condition "A.hasNetclass('SENSITIVE') && (B.hasNetclass('HIGHFREQ') || B.hasNetclass('MIDFREQ'))")
  (constraint clearance (min 0.45mm))
)

# --- High-speed buses --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A bus is the set of nets of ONE peripheral instance: SPI0_MOSI + SPI0_MISO +
# SPI0_CLK, or all the DDR nets, or all the USB nets. Chip selects, enables and
# supplies are NOT bus members — put those in SENSITIVE.
#
# Nets of the same bus may run close together, because their transitions happen at
# almost the same time. The 3x clearance applies between the bus and everything
# outside it. That asymmetry is what the negated clause below buys: without it, a
# flat HIGHFREQ clearance rule reports every intra-bus pair as a violation.
#
# This rule is a template naming a netclass that probably does not exist yet. That is
# safe: a rule referencing an unknown netclass simply never matches, and does not
# invalidate the rest of the file (verified on 10.0.6 — the rule after it still fired).
#
# Bus names are per-board, so this is a template. For each bus:
#   1. Create a netclass BUS_<INSTANCE> in Project Settings > Net Classes.
#   2. Assign its members with netclass_patterns, one pattern per net, using the
#      full net name including the sheet path. Enumerate the members rather than
#      globbing SPI0_* — KiCAD patterns have no exclusion syntax, and a glob would
#      drag SPI0_CS into the bus.
#   3. Replace the plain "HIGHFREQ clearance" rule above with the rule below, and
#      add one more "&& !(A.hasNetclass(...) && B.hasNetclass(...))" clause per bus.
# Membership is additive, so a net stays in HIGHFREQ while also being in its bus.
#
# The exemption has to live INSIDE this rule's condition. Do not try to do it with a
# separate relaxed intra-bus rule and last-match-wins ordering: DRC evaluates a pair
# under both role assignments, so a flat "A.hasNetclass('HIGHFREQ')" rule still
# matches with the roles swapped. Measured on a real board, 29 intra-bus pairs:
#   exemption rule placed first -> 31 intra-bus violations (no effect)
#   exemption rule placed last  ->  2 intra-bus violations (leaks)
#   self-exempting condition    ->  0 intra-bus violations
# all three keeping bus-to-outside enforcement at ~489.

(rule "HIGHFREQ clearance outside the bus"
  (severity error)
  (condition "A.hasNetclass('HIGHFREQ') && !(A.hasNetclass('BUS_SPI0') && B.hasNetclass('BUS_SPI0'))")
  (constraint clearance (min 0.45mm))
)

# Intra-bus pairs fall through to the bus netclass clearance, which is what lets them
# run close. Set that netclass clearance to 2x the track width if you want a floor
# there too.
